@packages/core import { SigilCore }
@packages/compiler/#module.sigil import { SigilCompiler }

expand SigilCompiler {
  logic {
    CompilationTarget {
      Resolve a location target by matching its normalized file and one-based
      physical line plus UTF-16 code-unit column to a start-inclusive, end-exclusive
      component or expand range. Treat LF and CRLF as physical line boundaries.

      When no component or expand encloses the requested position, reject with
      COMPILER_INVALID_INVOCATION, return no CompilationReport, and emit a failed
      terminal event when transport remains writable.

      Resolve the selected component to one exact declaration identity before
      identifying its workspace root, evaluation skill, and initial navigation paths.

      Condense the resolved component dependency graph into strongly connected groups
      and topologically order those groups. Break ties among independent groups and
      members by normalized workspace-relative Sigil path, declaration start offset,
      then exact component name.

      Preserve SIGIL_IMPORT_CYCLE from deterministic resolution for every cyclic
      group, make the report red, and skip agentic evaluation for that affected group
      and its unavailable dependents. Continue independent acyclic groups in their
      deterministic order.
    }

    CompilationInvocation {
      Unless history is disabled, ask SigilCompilationHistoryStore for optional prior
      evidence and supply it to SigilCompilationReportProtocol without treating it as
      authority.

      Observe cancellation through evaluation and report construction. Immediately
      before requested export begins, or immediately before completed-event
      settlement when export is absent, cross one success linearization point after
      which cancellation no longer changes this invocation's outcome.

      When export is requested, delegate destination validation and atomic writing to
      SigilCompilationReportExporter after the authoritative CompilationReport is
      constructed and before completed settlement. Export rejection returns no report
      and emits a failed terminal event when transport remains writable.

      Cancellation observed before that point rejects with COMPILER_CANCELLED and
      performs no export. Cancellation observed after export begins is ignored for
      settlement: successful export completes, while export failure rejects with
      COMPILER_FAILED. A completed export never coexists with COMPILER_CANCELLED.
    }

    OneShotCompilation {
      Validate CompilationOptions and resolve CompilationTarget before acquiring
      workspace evidence. Delegate profile, requested-stage, focus mapping,
      dependency closure, evaluator availability, budgets, and capabilities to
      SigilCompilationProfile.

      Acquire the selected workspace through SigilCore read-only semantics and
      dispatch the resolved target, stages, capabilities, and optional prior report
      to SigilCompilationEvaluation. Never construct a writable selected-workspace
      capability.

      Delegate diagnostic identity, lifecycle comparison, trace schema, and
      authoritative report construction to SigilCompilationReportProtocol
      constructReport using the internal CompilationEvaluationResult and optional
      prior evidence.

      After report construction, cross the CompilationInvocation cancellation
      boundary and delegate requested export to SigilCompilationReportExporter.

      Delegate the required completed terminal event to
      SigilCompilationEventProtocol. Terminal transport failure rejects with
      COMPILER_FAILED, returns no report, and performs no history replacement.

      After completed is delivered and before returning, when history is enabled, ask
      SigilCompilationHistoryStore to replace history. History replacement failure
      reports a host warning but does not change the already completed settlement or
      returned report. When history is disabled, perform neither history operation.
      Preserve other dependency-owned failure codes without reconstructing dependency
      policy.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    DependencyDirection {
      SigilCompiler depends on SigilCore for deterministic workspace semantics.

      SigilCore does not depend on SigilCompiler, evaluation skills, agent adapters,
      CLI behavior, or editor behavior.
    }

    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Compilation does not modify Sigil source, implementation source, configuration,
      tests, documentation, or other selected-workspace content.

      Compilation does not generate code, execute implementation experiments, or
      claim general proof of implementation feasibility. Context-only stages may cite
      concrete repository evidence demonstrating a constraint on the proposed design.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    ReadOnlyCompilation {
      Decision: Keep compilation read-only, exclude general feasibility proof, code
      generation, and implementation experiments, and permit repository-demonstrated
      feasibility constraints as design evidence.

      Scope: Governs every compilation stage and adapter.

      Trade-offs: The compiler cannot empirically validate whether a proposed
      contract can be implemented, but may report a concrete constraint already
      demonstrated by accessible repository evidence.

      Consequences: Compilation evaluates contracts and repository evidence while
      implementation validation remains a downstream team responsibility; context-
      only evidence never becomes a general feasibility attestation.
    }

    LocationBasedCompilation {
      Decision: Represent an editor selection as a file-backed source position and
      resolve its enclosing component or expand through deterministic Sigil ranges.

      Scope: Governs location targets across compiler, CLI, and editor compilation;
      excludes workspace and explicit file-wide targets.

      Trade-offs: The public target and CLI option contracts gain source-position
      fields.

      Design issues addressed: Name-only selection cannot distinguish duplicate
      components and textual prefix scanning cannot identify expand-only files.

      Discarded alternatives: Editor-side Sigil parsing and name-only component
      selection were rejected because they duplicate semantics or lose component
      identity.

      Consequences: A cursor resolves to one compiler-verified component identity
      without duplicating parsing in the editor.
    }

    ComponentCenteredCompilation {
      Decision: Make components the primary compilation unit and resolve file and
      workspace targets into component sets.

      Scope: Governs target resolution across CLI, editor, and CI surfaces.

      Consequences: A component cannot appear green while a matching expand or
      relevant relationship remains unevaluated.
    }

    DeterministicWorkspaceScheduling {
      Decision: Schedule strongly connected dependency groups topologically and use
      normalized path, declaration offset, and exact name as deterministic tie-
      breakers.

      Scope: Governs workspace-target component scheduling; excludes stage dependency
      order and evaluator-internal inspection order.

      Trade-offs: Cyclic groups cannot receive agentic evaluation even when
      independent acyclic groups can continue.

      Design issues addressed: Dependency order alone does not order unrelated
      components and cannot topologically order a cycle.

      Discarded alternatives: Discovery order and aborting the entire workspace on
      one cycle were rejected as unstable or unnecessarily broad.

      Consequences: Repeated workspace runs schedule identical evidence identically,
      while SIGIL_IMPORT_CYCLE remains an explicit red deterministic finding.
    }

    CompilationCancellationBoundary {
      Decision: Linearize successful compilation immediately before optional export,
      or immediately before completed-event settlement when export is absent.

      Scope: Governs cancellation races with evaluation, report construction,
      explicit export, and completed settlement.

      Trade-offs: Cancellation arriving after export begins cannot stop that export
      or replace its eventual success or failure.

      Design issues addressed: One invocation must not expose a completed export and
      COMPILER_CANCELLED for the same run.

      Discarded alternatives: Allowing cancellation throughout atomic replacement was
      rejected because destination state and method settlement could diverge.

      Consequences: Pre-linearization cancellation performs no export; post-
      linearization cancellation leaves export and completion deterministic.
    }

    OneShotCompilationOrchestration {
      Decision: Coordinate one-shot compilation through a single ordered
      orchestration concept while delegating each semantic and storage policy to its
      owning component.

      Scope: Governs invocation validation, profile and stage resolution, read-only
      workspace access, evaluation dispatch, report construction, optional history
      and export, and terminal settlement; excludes dependency-internal policy and
      reusable compilation sessions.

      Trade-offs: The compiler owns an explicit sequence across several cohesive
      collaborators.

      Design issues addressed: Hosts need one authoritative owner for preparing and
      settling a one-shot compilation without moving dependency policies into the
      module index.

      Discarded alternatives: CLI-owned orchestration and implicit coordination
      spread across report, evaluation, and storage components were rejected.

      Consequences: Every host invokes the same sequence while component-specific
      policy remains independently changeable.
    }

    PostTerminalHistoryReplacement {
      Decision: Replace optional compilation history only after the completed
      terminal event is delivered and before the invocation returns.

      Scope: Governs one-shot history replacement ordering; excludes history storage
      policy and reusable compilation sessions.

      Trade-offs: Process exit after terminal delivery can leave optional history
      stale even though the caller received the authoritative report.

      Design issues addressed: A failed or undeliverable invocation must not become
      prior evidence for a later compilation.

      Discarded alternatives: Pre-terminal history replacement and compensating
      rollback were rejected because terminal delivery can still fail and rollback
      adds another fallible state transition.

      Consequences: Completed delivery is the success linearization for history;
      history-write failure remains a warning and does not change the returned
      report.
    }
  }

  cases {
    CompilationTarget {
      Compiling one component includes every matching expand even when those expands
      live in other files.

      Compiling a position inside an expand resolves the component represented by
      that expand.

      A position on the first character of a component or expand range and a position
      on its closing delimiter select it. The position immediately after the closing
      delimiter does not.

      LF and CRLF each advance one physical line. A column counts one-based UTF-16
      code units and a position on a line-ending sequence selects no delimiter text.

      A position outside every component and expand rejects with
      COMPILER_INVALID_INVOCATION, returns no report, and emits a failed terminal
      event when transport remains writable.

      Independent components use normalized path, declaration offset, and exact name
      order. A cyclic dependency group produces SIGIL_IMPORT_CYCLE, is skipped for
      agentic evaluation, and makes the report red while independent acyclic groups
      continue.
    }

    CompilationInvocation {
      Cancellation during evaluation or report construction returns
      COMPILER_CANCELLED and performs no export.

      Cancellation after export begins does not alter settlement. Successful export
      completes; failed export rejects with COMPILER_FAILED; neither returns
      COMPILER_CANCELLED.
    }

    OneShotCompilation {
      Omitting requestedStage and focus evaluates the complete selected profile.
      Selecting design evaluates architecture-design and its dependency closure;
      selecting implementation evaluates current-code-compatibility and its
      dependency closure.

      Supplying both requestedStage and focus rejects with
      COMPILER_INVALID_INVOCATION before workspace access or evaluation.

      Unavailable required evaluators reject with
      COMPILER_PROFILE_EVALUATORS_REQUIRED, return no report, and emit a failed event
      when transport remains writable.

      disableHistory performs no history read or replacement. Successful one-shot
      evaluation returns an internal result, constructs one authoritative report,
      performs requested export, emits the same report in completed, attempts non-
      authoritative history replacement, and returns it.

      Cancellation before linearization, evaluation rejection, report-construction
      rejection, or export rejection performs no history replacement. History-write
      failure after completed delivery warns but does not change the returned report.

      Failure writing the completed terminal event rejects with COMPILER_FAILED and
      returns no report even when report construction and export succeeded; history
      replacement is not attempted.
    }
  }
}
